<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José de San Martín</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José de San Martín was an Argentine general who led the liberation of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> across the mountains into Chile, where with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a> he won at Chacabuco in 1817 and Maipú in 1818. He then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, proclaimed its independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1821 and ruled as Protector before withdrawing in 1822.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin.jpg" alt="José de San Martín" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The exact author is disputed. Some sources attribute it to Jean Baptiste Madou, others to the art teacher of San Martin's … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_de_San_Mart%C3%ADn_(retrato,_c.1828).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
